{
  "dom_id": "step-q90821",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14274151/assets/910b0928aaf61b43acae11b73e92253fe90291ce8cedf9f54e7f9f526f3e0e70.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above? \n\nI. $f(5)=1$\n\nII. $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n\nIII. The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. There is no arrow from $5\\in X$ to $1\\in Y.$ So, $f(5)\\ne 1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the diagram, $1\\in Y$ (and also $9\\in Y$) has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $1\\in Y.$ As a result, there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=1$ and $y=9$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x)\\ne y$ for any $x$ in $X.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
